Scan once while storing previously seen values and their indices. Before storing the current value, check whether its complement has already appeared.
Code (python):
def two_sum(values, target):
    seen = {}
    for index, value in enumerate(values):
        complement = target - value
        if complement in seen:
            return [seen[complement], index]
        seen[value] = index
    return []
Expected output: two_sum([2, 7, 11, 15], 9) returns [0, 1]
Time complexity: O(n) average
Space complexity: O(n)

Maintain inclusive low and high boundaries. Compare the middle element, then discard the half that cannot contain the target.
Code (python):
def binary_search(values, target):
    low, high = 0, len(values) - 1
    while low <= high:
        middle = low + (high - low) // 2
        if values[middle] == target:
            return middle
        if values[middle] < target:
            low = middle + 1
        else:
            high = middle - 1
    return -1
Expected output: binary_search([2, 4, 7, 10], 7) returns 2
Time complexity: O(log n)
Space complexity: O(1)

Use two inclusive bounds and compute the midpoint without adding low and high directly, avoiding integer overflow for large indexes.
Code (java):
static int binarySearch(int[] values, int target) {
    int low = 0, high = values.length - 1;
    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        if (values[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (values[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
        else high = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}
Expected output: binarySearch(new int[]{2, 4, 7, 10}, 7) returns 2
Time complexity: O(log n)
Space complexity: O(1)

Initialize the maximum from the first element, then scan the remaining elements and update it when a larger value is found. The caller must provide a valid non-empty array.
Code (c):
int max_value(const int values[], int length) {
    int maximum = values[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < length; ++i) {
        if (values[i] > maximum) maximum = values[i];
    }
    return maximum;
}
Expected output: max_value([3, 9, 2], 3) returns 9
Time complexity: O(n)
Space complexity: O(1)

